orption and enterohepatic recycling of raloxifene. The mechanism has not been described, but is probably due to a binding phenomenon occurring in the intestine. A similar reaction may occur with colestipol, although data is lacking.","mechanismSource":"MecDDI"},"DDInter387":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter393":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter398":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter413":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter437":{"severity":"Moderate","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter449":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter484":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter506":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter513":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter534":{"severity":"","severitySource":"","mechanism":"Raloxifene is more than 95% protein bound. Caution should be exercised if raloxifene is coadministered with other highly protein-bound drugs. The mechanism is displacement of the drug from its protein-binding site by raloxifene. Displacement may lead to increased free plasma concentrations and potentiate toxicity of drugs affected by raloxifene. Formal in-vitro studies indicate that raloxifene did not affect the binding of warfarin, phenytoin, or tamoxifen. The clinician may want to use caution when raloxifene is used with other highly-protein bound drugs, such as diazepam, diazoxide, or lidocaine.","mechanismSource":"MecDDI"},"DDInter535":{"severity":"Minor","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"Raloxifene is more than 95% protein bound. Caution should be exercised if raloxifene is coadministered with other highly protein-bound drugs. The mechanism is displacement of the drug from its protein-binding site by raloxifene. Displacement may lead to increased free plasma concentrations and potentiate toxicity of drugs affected by raloxifene. Formal in-vitro studies indicate that raloxifene did not affect the binding of warfarin, phenytoin, or tamoxifen. The clinician may want to use caution when raloxifene is used with other highly-protein bound drugs, such as diazepam, diazoxide, or lidocaine.","mechanismSource":"MecDDI"},"DDInter537":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter543":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter578":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter593":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter596":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter652":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter657":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter667":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter681":{"severity":"Moderate","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter692":{"severity":"Moderate","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter712":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter732":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter736":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter766":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"DDInter","mechanism":"","mechanismSource":""},"DDInter826":{"severity":"Unknown","severitySource":"D